\section{Basic concepts}
\begin{definition}
An {\em inner-product space} with complex scalars, $/mathbf{C}$, is a vector space $V$ with complex scalars, together with a complex-valued function 
$<v, w>$, called the inner product, defined on $V \times V$, which has the following properties:
\begin{itemize}
\item (1) For all $v \in  V, <v, v> \geq 0.
\item (2) If <v, v> = 0 then v = 0.
\item (3) For all $v$ and $w$ in $V$, $$<v, w> = <w, v>$$.
\item (4) For all v_1, v_2 and $w$ in $V, $$<v_1 + v, w> = <v_1, w> + <v_2, w>$$.
\item (5) For all $v, w \in V$, and all scalars a, one has that 
$$<av,w>= a<v, w>$$.(The inner product is linear in the first variable, and conjugate linear in the second.)
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}


\section{Hilbert space}
\begin{definition}
 A {\em Hilbert space} is an inner product space which is complete as a metric space, that is for every sequence {v_n} of vectors in $V$, if 
$||v_m − v_n|| \to 0$ as $m$ and $n$ both tend to infinity, there is in $V$, a
vector $v_{\omega} \in V$ such that $||v_n - v_{\omega}|| \to 0$ as $n \to \infty}$. 
\end{definition}

 Sequences with the property that $$lim _{m \to \infty, n \to \infty} ||v_m−v_n|| = 0$$ are called {\em Cauchy sequences}. Usually one works with Hilbert spaces because one needs to have available limits of Cauchy sequences. Finite dimensional inner product spaces are automatically Hilbert spaces.
However, it is the infinite dimensional Hilbert spaces that are important 
for the proper foundation of Quantum Mechanics.
